{
  "id": "T-288",
  "milestone": "M2",
  "depends_on": [],
  "title": "Spec preflight reads each spec fresh, asks only whether it is clear, right and simple, and allows 3 passes per task",
  "public_title": "Make spec preflight a single fresh review",
  "scope": [
    "bin/lib/**",
    "bin/fm-review.sh",
    "bin/fm-herdr.py",
    "bin/fm-canary.sh",
    "skills/reviewer/SKILL.md",
    "skills/firstmate/SKILL.md",
    "skills/firstmate/rule-inventory.json",
    "design/design.md",
    "tests/**",
    "design/tasks/T-288.json"
  ],
  "acceptance": [
    "Why. Today every re-review of a spec makes the preflight reviewer copy its earlier numbered list again, keep each number, move each item through fixed states (gap to done or open, ok to ok or open) and label new items. One slip in that copying voids the whole pass. Four specs drafted on 2026-10-11 stopped on this copying alone (their signed preflight records are in firstmate's local evidence store, `state/evidence/<project>/<task>/`), and the carried list kept asking short specs to put implementation detail back. The captain decided that a spec states only what, why and when it is done; how is the worker's job, checked by pull request review.",
    "What, the question. The preflight reviewer reads the spec with the code at hand and answers four questions: is the spec clear, is it the right thing to build, is there a simpler way, and does it contradict the code or another rule. It does not ask for implementation detail such as callers or line numbers. The firstmate skill rule that a rule-changing task must say what happens to existing records stays, and the reviewer still checks that such a spec says it. Design check 5 also stays: the reviewer still checks that a spec listing `design/design.md` names the numbered section it edits.",
    "What, the answer. The reviewer writes a free-form review. Its last line is `SPEC-OK:<task>` or `SPEC-GAPS:<task>`, exactly as today; the numbered list and the `PREFLIGHT-COMPLETE:<task>` line are no longer required. Each gap names the acceptance line it concerns and suggests a rewrite. A reviewer rewrite of the spec text still goes through today's accepted-rewrite path.",
    "What, every pass is a first pass. A preflight prompt holds no earlier list, and the answer is checked with no comparison to an earlier answer. The code and tests that exist only for the carried list (carried numbers, state moves, amendment labels, one item per acceptance line) are deleted. These rules stay: bytes that once got SPEC-GAPS cannot get SPEC-OK, and rewrite reasons refused earlier still go into the next prompt. The description in `design/design.md` §6, under the heading \"Spec preflight and migration (T-185)\", changes to match, and the rule inventory entries for every SKILL.md rule this task changes are updated.",
    "What, old receipts. A receipt is the signed record a preflight writes; it binds its verdict, and so SPEC-OK, to the exact spec bytes it judged. Receipts written before this change stay readable and keep binding their bytes; a pin or repin that relied on one is unaffected.",
    "What, a pass budget. A pass is a preflight that wrote a receipt with a verdict. A task gets at most 3 passes after its last captain budget answer, counting only receipts written after this change merges. A fourth pass is refused, and the captain gets a card with three choices: narrow the spec, park the task, or allow one more pass. Any answer resets the count. For an external project the card names no private text, as the review round budget card does today.",
    "What stays. Pin and repin still require SPEC-OK on the exact spec bytes.",
    "Done, test one. A stub reviewer whose free-form answer has no numbered items ends with `SPEC-OK:<task>`, and a short spec reaches SPEC-OK in one pass (`preflight: short spec passes in one pass`). It fails on the base.",
    "Done, test two. A pass after an edit has no earlier list in its prompt and is judged on the new bytes alone (`preflight: re-pass judges new bytes only`). It fails on the base.",
    "Done, test three. A fourth pass raises the budget card and runs no reviewer, and a captain answer allows the next pass; a task that already has 3 or more receipts from before this change still gets a pass (`preflight: fourth pass raises budget card`). It fails on the base.",
    "Done, test four. A receipt written before this change still authorizes a pin (`preflight: old receipt still authorizes pin`). It is a regression check.",
    "Done, test five. An answer whose last line is not `SPEC-OK:<task>` or `SPEC-GAPS:<task>` is refused with a message that names the expected line (`preflight: malformed final line refused`). It is a regression check.",
    "Out of scope. The pull request review and its round budget; how specs are written; the planned design review of pages."
  ],
